    "text": "Imagine you have a cold drink on a hot day. You want to keep it cold, right? A refrigerator works in a similar way to keep your food and drinks cold. \n\nInside the fridge, there's a special liquid called a refrigerant. This liquid can change from a liquid to a gas easily. The fridge uses this liquid to take heat away from the food and transfer it outside.\n\nHere's how it works: \n\n1. The refrigerant liquid goes through a tiny tube called a coil, which is usually at the back or bottom of the fridge. \n2. As it goes through the coil, it absorbs heat from the food inside the fridge and turns into a gas.\n3. The gas then goes to a compressor, which is like a strong pump. The compressor squeezes the gas, making it hot.\n4. The hot gas then goes to another coil, usually outside the fridge or at the back. This coil is called the condenser coil.\n5. As the hot gas goes through the condenser coil, it releases its heat to the air outside the fridge and turns back into a liquid.\n6. The liquid refrigerant then goes back to the coil inside the fridge, and the process starts all over again.\n\nThis process keeps happening, taking heat",
